  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nPlenty of sunshine across the Upper Ohio Valley is allowing\ndestabilization to take place ahead of a MCV tracking across\nsouthern Ohio. This will provide the support for lift along with\nan advancing prefrontal trough. By 18Z, MLCAPE increases into \nthe 1000-1500 J/kg range at least according to RAP/REFS. This\nbuoyancy will allow convection to increase in coverage during\nthe early to middle afternoon hours. CAMs continue to support\nthese ideas, with the stronger convection moving east of the\nregion by 02Z-03Z, with a few lingering showers crossing with\nthe main surface cold front shortly thereafter.\n\nThe main severe risk remains damaging gusts. DCAPE is still \npegged to remain in the 600-800 J/kg range and thus be \nmarginally favorable for downbursts, and the best\n0-6km/effective shear remains further down the Ohio Valley. So,\nwe are likely not looking at a higher-end threat. Still, with\ninitial discrete storms likely to conglomerate over time in \nmulticell clusters/short lines, enough cold pool consolidation \nmay occur to create some scattered instances of 55-65 MPH wind \ngusts given the healthy MLCAPE expectations. Large hail would be\na secondary threat given the relatively weak shear and mid-\nlevel lapse rates, but may be possible in the strongest storms. \nDownpours/locally heavy rainfall totals remain possible as well,\nbut the HREF suggests the greatest QPF potential lies just to \nour north and west closer to Lake Erie, and much of the I-80 \ncorridor has higher flash flood guidance values than to the \nsouth. Precipitable water values are not excessively high \n(75-85th percentile) as well. Will continue to monitor, but will\nhold off on a Flood Watch for now.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMid-level troughing and cold advection lingers over the region \non Sunday, which may support isolated/scattered shower and\nthunderstorms development during the peak heating hours of \nSunday, mainly north of I-70. Although decreasing overall \nmoisture depth should keep a cap on coverage, one or two strong\nto severe storms may be possible given the cooling temperatures\naloft, if around 1000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE can develop as\nhi-res models suggest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nShowers may linger north of I-80 and along the ridges (a cool-\nseason type of setup) into Monday morning with ongoing cool\nnorthwest flow over a warm Lake Erie.\n\nOtherwise, high pressure and height rises aloft point to a\nprolonged (relatively speaking, in relation to the previous\nmonth) dry period to start next week. High temperature does\nstart below seasonal averages on Monday before rising back near\naverage by mid week. Long range ensembles currently favor\nThursday as the next day with showers/thunderstorms as the next\nupper trough crosses.",
